he system has explicitly mapped “2026 Spring Festival” to **FW21 = 2026-02-15 to 2026-02-21**, we use exact match:  \\n  `FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\'`  \\n  (Note: This is safer than range-based overlap logic because the fiscal week is atomic and pre-validated.)  \\n- **GROUP BY**: `Channel`  \\n- **Aggregation**: `SUM(ATTN)`  \\n- **Ordering**: Not specified, but best practice is to order by attendance descending for readability.  \\n\\n3. **Implementation Plan**:  \\nA single SQL query suffices. Structure:  \\n```sql\\nSELECT Channel, SUM(ATTN) AS total_attendance  \\nFROM survey_attendance_mock  \\nWHERE FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\'  \\nGROUP BY Channel  \\nORDER BY total_attendance DESC;\\n```  \\nNo JOINs, no subqueries, no dynamic date calculation — all time logic is fixed and validated per the system’s pre-computed fiscal week mapping.\\n\\n【SQL Generation】\\n```sql\\nSELECT Channel, ROUND(SUM(ATTN), 0) AS total_attendance FROM survey_attendance_mock WHERE FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\' GROUP BY Channel ORDER BY total_attendance DESC LIMIT 10\\n```\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-03T01:33:09.045022\"}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Compare park attendance across all channels during the Spring Festival periods of 2025 and 2026\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-03T01:34:53.838251\"}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"stages\": [{\"id\": \"intent_recognition\", \"name\": \"Intent Recognition\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"Intent recognized: Smart Query\\nConfidence: 0.95\", \"metadata\": {\"intent\": \"smart_query\", \"reason\": \"\", \"confidence\": 0.95, \"intent_name\": \"Smart Query\"}, \"collapsed\": false, \"timestamp\": 1772472803331}, {\"id\": \"table_selection\", \"name\": \"Table Selection\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"The user has already selected the table \'survey_attendance_mock\', which is the only table available for selection. The current question asks for a year-over-year comparison of park attendance across channels during the 2025 and 2026 Spring Festival periods. This requires temporal filtering (to isolate Spring Festival weeks in both years), channel grouping (Channel), and attendance metrics (ATTN and ATTN_LY). The table provides fiscal year (FY_N), fiscal week date boundaries (FW_START_DATE, FW_END_DATE), and year-over-year attendance (ATTN_LY), making it fully sufficient. No other tables are available or needed per the constraints. The historical context (\'Park Attendance Across All Channels During the 2026 Spring Festival\') further confirms this table’s suitability and reinforces the focus on attendance and channel dimensions.\", \"metadata\": {\"reason\": \"The user has already selected the table \'survey_attendance_mock\', which is the only table available for selection. The current question asks for a year-over-year comparison of park attendance across channels during the 2025 and 2026 Spring Festival periods. This requires temporal filtering (to isolate Spring Festival weeks in both years), channel grouping (Channel), and attendance metrics (ATTN and ATTN_LY). The table provides fiscal year (FY_N), fiscal week date boundaries (FW_START_DATE, FW_END_DATE), and year-over-year attendance (ATTN_LY), making it fully sufficient. No other tables are available or needed per the constraints. The historical context (\'Park Attendance Across All Channels During the 2026 Spring Festival\') further confirms this table’s suitability and reinforces the focus on attendance and channel dimensions.\", \"selected_tables\": [\"502827a4-cc66-46a5-b3eb-e940cf16cb3d\"]}, \"collapsed\": false, \"timestamp\": 1772472809848}, {\"id\": \"stage_clarification\", \"name\": \"Intent Clarification\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"Analysis complete, intent is clear.\", \"metadata\": {\"analysis\": {\"hasTimeInfo\": false, \"isAmbiguous\": false, \"hasFilterInfo\": false, \"hasMetricInfo\": false, \"hasDimensionInfo\": false}, \"progress\": 0.5, \"needsClarification\": false, \"clarificationQuestions\": []}, \"collapsed\": false, \"timestamp\": 1772472823271}, {\"id\": \"stage_thinking\", \"name\": \"Model Thinking\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"1. **Problem Analysis**:  \\nThe user wants to compare park attendance across all sales channels during the Spring Festival periods of 2025 and 2026. This is a comparative time-series analysis across two distinct, non-overlapping holiday windows — not relative time expressions like “last week” or “this month”, but fixed, pre-resolved fiscal week date ranges tied to the Spring Festival.  \\n\\nKey facts from the pre-resolved temporal context (highest-priority instruction):  \\n- 2025 Spring Festival period corresponds to FY2025 FW18: **2025-01-26 to 2025-02-01** (7 days, Sunday–Saturday)  \\n- 2026 Spring Festival period corresponds to FY2026 FW21: **2026-02-15 to 2026-02-21** (7 days, Sunday–Saturday)  \\nThese dates are authoritative, database-verified fiscal week boundaries — no inference, no lunar calendar logic, no knowledge-base lookup needed.  \\n\\nThe query targets:  \\n- **Metric**: `ATTN` (park attendance /入园人数)  \\n- **Dimension**: `Channel` (sales channel), with all values (e.g., \'3rd party\', \'COMP\', \'Consumer Direct\', \'Niche\', \'unknown\') — no filtering applied; “across all channels” means full enumeration  \\n- **Time filter**: Two explicit, disjoint date ranges — must be included in WHERE or CASE logic  \\n- **Aggregation**: Summed attendance per channel, separately for each year’s Spring Festival period  \\n- **Output requirement**: Side-by-side comparison — either as parallel columns (2025 vs 2026) or stacked rows with a period label  \\n\\nNo historical context is inherited beyond the *topic* (“Park Attendance During the 2026 Spring Festival”) — but this current question explicitly adds the 2025 comparison, so no prior time filters apply. The only time constraints are the two fixed Spring Festival windows.\\n\\n2. **Solution Approach**:  \\nThis is a **comparative aggregation query**, best implemented using a single SQL statement with conditional aggregation (`CASE WHEN`) to avoid duplication and ensure consistent grouping.  \\n\\n- **Table**: `survey_attendance_mock` (only table provided and relevant)  \\n- **Critical fields**:  \\n  - `Channel`: grouping dimension (VARCHAR)  \\n  - `ATTN`: numeric metric to sum  \\n  - `FW_START_DATE` and `FW_END_DATE`: used for date-range filtering (note: each row represents a fiscal week, so the entire week must fall *within* the target period — but since the pre-resolved ranges *are* exact fiscal weeks, we can safely use `FW_START_DATE BETWEEN ... AND ...` or match `FW_START_DATE = \'YYYY-MM-DD\'`. However, the instruction says “use the bracketed dates”, and the table has `FW_START_DATE` and `FW_END_DATE`, so safest condition is:  \\n    `(FW_START_DATE >= \'2025-01-26\' AND FW_END_DATE <= \'2025-02-01\') OR (FW_START_DATE >= \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE <= \'2026-02-21\')`  \\n    But note: the pre-resolved ranges *are* full fiscal weeks — and the table’s `FW_START_DATE`/`FW_END_DATE` exactly represent those weeks. So simpler and more precise:  \\n    `FW_START_DATE IN (\'2025-01-26\', \'2026-02-15\')` — because each row corresponds to one fiscal week, and those two start dates uniquely identify the two target weeks.  \\n\\n- **WHERE clause**: Must restrict to only these two fiscal weeks — using `FW_START_DATE IN (\'2025-01-26\', \'2026-02-15\')` ensures precision and avoids accidental inclusion of partial weeks.  \\n- **GROUP BY**: `Channel` (to show attendance per channel)  \\n- **Aggregation**: Two `SUM(CASE...)` expressions — one for 2025, one for 2026 — to produce aligned columns.  \\n- **Ordering**: Not specified, but logical to `ORDER BY Channel` for readability.  \\n\\n3. **Implementation Plan**:  \\nA single, efficient SQL query suffices. Use conditional aggregation to compute both yearly sums in one pass. The structure will be:  \\n\\n```sql\\nSELECT \\n  Channel,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END) AS ATTN_2025_SpringFestival,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END) AS ATTN_2026_SpringFestival,\\n  -- Optional: % change or delta could be added, but not requested\\nFROM survey_attendance_mock\\nWHERE FW_START_DATE IN (\'2025-01-26\', \'2026-02-15\')\\nGROUP BY Channel\\nORDER BY Channel;\\n```  \\n\\nThis approach guarantees correctness (uses only the two verified fiscal week start dates), avoids ambiguity (no date-range overlap or boundary errors), supports “all channels” without hardcoding, and delivers direct comparison in tabular form. No UNION ALL is needed — conditional aggregation is cleaner and more performant for this 2-period comparison.\", \"metadata\": {\"progress\": 0.6, \"thinking_complete\": true}, \"collapsed\": false, \"timestamp\": 1772472833073}, {\"id\": \"sql_generation\", \"name\": \"SQL Generation\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"```sql\\nSELECT Channel, ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END), 0) AS ATTN_2025_SpringFestival, ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END), 0) AS ATTN_2026_SpringFestival FROM survey_attendance_mock WHERE FW_START_DATE IN (\'2025-01-26\', \'2026-02-15\') GROUP BY Channel ORDER BY Channel\\n```\", \"metadata\": {\"sql\": \"SELECT Channel, ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END), 0) AS ATTN_2025_SpringFestival, ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN ATTN ELSE 0 END), 0) AS ATTN_2026_SpringFestival FROM survey_attendance_mock WHERE FW_START_DATE IN (\'2025-01-26\', \'2026-02-15\') GROUP BY Channel ORDER BY Channel\", \"retry_count\": 0}, \"collapsed\": false, \"timestamp\": 1772472861792}, {\"id\": \"stage_execute\", \"name\": \"SQL Execution\", \"status\": \"completed\", \"content\": \"Query complete: 5 re