icable since we’re explicitly comparing two distinct years, not using built-in lag fields).  \\n- Since the request is “compare the two years”, the output must enable side-by-side analysis — meaning either: (a) two rows (one per year) with identical dimensions/metrics, or (b) columns showing 2025 and 2026 values side by side. Given SQL constraints, the standard approach is to use conditional aggregation or UNION — but here, simpler is better: select both periods with a year label and aggregate per year.\\n\\n2. **Solution Approach**:  \\nThis is a **time-based comparative aggregation query**, requiring:  \\n- Single-table usage: `survey_attendance_mock`.  \\n- Key fields: `FY_N` (to verify year context), `FW_START_DATE`, `FW_END_DATE`, `ATTN`, `Sample_Size`, and optionally `Channel` or other dimensions if breakdown is implied — but the question says “compare the two years” without specifying granularity, so base-level annual-holiday totals are appropriate.  \\n- WHERE condition must filter *exactly* the two fiscal weeks:  \\n  - One week: `FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2025-02-01\'`  \\n  - Second week: `FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\'`  \\n  (Note: These are exact date matches — not ranges — because each fiscal week has unique start/end in the table; this avoids overlap or ambiguity.)  \\n- To enable comparison, we’ll add a computed column `Year` (e.g., `\'2025\'` or `\'2026\'`) and GROUP BY it.  \\n- Aggregation: `SUM(ATTN)` for total attendance, `SUM(Sample_Size)` for total sample, and optionally `COUNT(*)` to confirm row count consistency.  \\n- No JOINs needed — all required data resides in one table.  \\n- No additional filtering (e.g., Channel, region) is inherited or implied — the prior queries were *for the same table and same metric*, but no persistent filter was applied beyond time; thus, we retain full scope.\\n\\n3. **Implementation Plan**:  \\nA single SQL query suffices. Structure:  \\n- SELECT clause includes `CASE WHEN` or direct date-based labeling to assign `\'2025\'` / `\'2026\'`, plus `SUM(ATTN)`, `SUM(Sample_Size)`.  \\n- WHERE uses OR logic to include only the two exact fiscal weeks.  \\n- GROUP BY the year label to produce two rows.  \\n- ORDER BY year ascending for clarity.  \\n- Optional: Include `AVG(ATTN)` or per-capita if needed — but user didn’t specify metrics beyond “compare”, and `ATTN` is the primary attendance measure per the prior queries.  \\n\\nFinal SQL will look like:  \\n```sql\\nSELECT \\n  CASE \\n    WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN \'2025\'\\n    WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN \'2026\'\\n  END AS Year,\\n  ROUND(SUM(ATTN), 0) AS total_attendance,\\n  SUM(Sample_Size) AS total_sample_size\\nFROM survey_attendance_mock\\nWHERE (FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2025-02-01\')\\n   OR (FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\')\\nGROUP BY \\n  CASE \\n    WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN \'2025\'\\n    WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN \'2026\'\\n  END\\nORDER BY Year;\\n```\\n\\n【SQL Generation】\\n```sql\\nSELECT CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN \'2025\' WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN \'2026\' END AS Year, ROUND(SUM(ATTN), 0) AS total_attendance, SUM(Sample_Size) AS total_sample_size FROM survey_attendance_mock WHERE (FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2025-02-01\') OR (FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' AND FW_END_DATE = \'2026-02-21\') GROUP BY CASE WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2025-01-26\' THEN \'2025\' WHEN FW_START_DATE = \'2026-02-15\' THEN \'2026\' END ORDER BY Year\\n```\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-03T10:20:28.422246\"}]',5,37,0,'2026-03-03 10:10:29','2026-03-03 10:20:28','2026-03-03 10:20:28',NULL,NULL,1,30);
